@prefix : <#> .
@prefix schema: <http://schema.org/> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

: a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:about :entityHrefCompanionTTL .

:entityHrefCompanionTTL a schema:HowTo ;
    schema:name "Entity Href Sourcing from Companion TTL"@en ;
    schema:description "All entity hyperlinks in generated HTML infographics must be resolver-wrapped IRIs sourced from the companion TTL published at its DAV path — never external Wikipedia, W3C, schema.org, or GitHub IRIs for entities defined in the companion TTL."@en ;
    schema:dateCreated "2026-06-14T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:dateModified "2026-07-30T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    schema:step :step1, :step2, :step3, :step4, :step5, :step6, :step7 .

:step1 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 1 ;
    schema:name "Derive the DAV base IRI from the skills contract"@en ;
    schema:text """Read the DAV path pattern from rdf-infographic-skill/SKILL.md or kg-generator/SKILL.md:
  {default-resolver-server}/DAV/demos/daas/{filename}.ttl

Substitute the companion TTL filename (no path, no leading slash).
NEVER ask the user for this path — it is derivable from the skills contract."""@en .

:step2 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 2 ;
    schema:name "Pre-build gate: grep companion TTL for entity hash IRIs"@en ;
    schema:text """Before writing any entity href, grep the companion .ttl file for the entity name.

Pattern to look for:
  :{EntityHashID} a schema:{Type} ;

Examples:
  :DimIdentity, :DimQueryability, :AdvSPARQL, :StepExtractYAML

If the entity exists → proceed to Step 3.
If the entity does NOT exist → add it to the companion TTL first, then link."""@en .

:step3 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 3 ;
    schema:name "Build the resolver-wrapped href"@en ;
    schema:text """Resolver pattern:
  {default-resolver-server}/describe/?url={url-encoded-entity-IRI}

Entity IRI = DAV base IRI + '#' + hash fragment
Example:
  base:    {default-resolver-server}/DAV/demos/daas/okf-vs-rdf-linked-data-comparison.ttl
  entity:  #DimIdentity
  full:    {default-resolver-server}/DAV/demos/daas/okf-vs-rdf-linked-data-comparison.ttl#DimIdentity
  encoded: https%3A%2F%2Flinkeddata.uriburner.com%2FDAV%2Fdemos%2Fdaas%2Fokf-vs-rdf-linked-data-comparison.ttl%23DimIdentity
  href:    {default-resolver-server}/describe/?url=https%3A%2F%2Flinkeddata.uriburner.com%2FDAV%2Fdemos%2Fdaas%2Fokf-vs-rdf-linked-data-comparison.ttl%23DimIdentity

NEVER fabricate resolver URLs. Compute the percent-encoding manually or with a tool."""@en .

:step4 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 4 ;
    schema:name "Scope: what gets companion TTL hrefs vs. intentional external IRIs"@en ;
    schema:text """MUST use companion TTL resolver hrefs:
  - Comparison table dimension cells (e.g., Entity Identity → #DimIdentity)
  - Advantages/features section headings (e.g., Global Identity → #AdvGlobalIdentity)
  - HowTo step headings and titles (e.g., #StepExtractYAML)
  - Any entity whose hash IRI exists or should exist in the companion TTL

INTENTIONAL external IRIs (leave unchanged):
  - External vocabulary terms (e.g., GitHub OKF ontology IRIs like #ConceptDocument) — defined in a third-party spec
  - FAQ/glossary entities from a dedicated FAQ TTL — reference their own TTL
  - W3C namespace predicates used as inline code examples (e.g., owl:sameAs) — these are predicates, not entity subjects
  - Product/tool attribution links in the footer — use canonical homepages, not the resolver"""@en .

:step5 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 5 ;
    schema:name "BLOCKING CHECK — bare-fragment hrefs silently resolve to nothing; verify before calling any document done"@en ;
    schema:text """Recurred 2026-07-02: a full report (FAQ, Glossary, HowTo — 41 static hrefs, plus 2 more in KG-explorer JS) was built with the resolver *wrapper* applied correctly everywhere, but the ?url= parameter itself was only ever '%23{fragment}' (a bare fragment, no scheme/host/path) — e.g. describe/?url=%23q0 decodes to describe/?url=#q0, which is meaningless input to the resolver. This is easy to miss: every link LOOKS resolver-wrapped at a glance (same {default-resolver-server}/describe/?url= prefix), and the document may already use the correct absolute DAV base elsewhere (SPARQL FROM clause, KG-explorer data-graph-iri) without that base ever being applied to entity hrefs. Do not assume applying the wrapper means the href is well-formed — decode and check.

This is now a MANDATORY BLOCKING CHECK, not an optional post-edit nicety, run before delivering any HTML infographic with resolver-linked entities:
  grep -c 'describe/?url=%23' file.html   # MUST be 0 — any match is a bare-fragment href with no base IRI
  grep -c 'linkeddata.uriburner.com/describe/?url=https%3A%2F%2Flinkeddata.uriburner.com%2FDAV' file.html   # should roughly match the count of internal entity hrefs

Also check runtime-constructed hrefs, not just static HTML: KG-explorer / D3 code often builds an `iriFor` or similar JS object mapping node IDs to IRIs — grep that object literally for bare `'#...'` string values (e.g. `:'#layerAuthentication'`), since those bypass static-HTML grep entirely and only surface as broken links when clicked.

Confirm zero stale external entity hrefs remain where companion TTL IRIs should be:
  grep -c 'href="https://en.wikipedia.org' file.html   # expect 0 for entities in companion TTL
  grep -c 'href="https://schema.org/' file.html         # expect 0 for entities in companion TTL
  grep -c 'href="https://www.w3.org/' file.html         # expect 0 for entities in companion TTL

If counts are wrong, re-read the companion TTL and repeat Steps 2–3."""@en .

:step6 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 6 ;
    schema:name "First plain-text occurrence of every named Person/Organization must be resolver-linked — not just some later occurrence"@en ;
    schema:text """Caught 2026-07-30 on ontologies-agentic-systems-latent-space-mesh-claude_sonnet_5-1.html: Frank Coyle, Emil Eifrem, Kingsley Idehen, and Prasenjit Sarkar were all resolver-linked correctly in their quote-card (Voices section) — but their EARLIER, first-in-document mentions in the hero paragraph and the intro paragraph were left as plain unlinked text. Neo4j, OpenLink Software, and UC Berkeley had the same defect: named in body prose but never linked anywhere.

RULE: when a person or organization already has an entity IRI in the companion TTL, its FIRST occurrence in reading order (hero > intro > body, before KG Explorer) must be the resolver-linked one, not a later occurrence. A later duplicate link does not fix an earlier unlinked mention — a reader encountering the name for the first time gets no link.

BLOCKING CHECK before delivery: for every named schema:Person / schema:Organization in the companion TTL, grep the HTML body (excluding <script> blocks) for that entity's literal name text, take the FIRST match, and confirm it is inside an <a href="{default-resolver-server}/describe/?url=..."> anchor. If the first match is plain text, fix that occurrence — do not just confirm a link exists somewhere later in the document."""@en .

:step7 a schema:HowToStep ;
    schema:position 7 ;
    schema:name "RDF/HTML entity parity — every entity minted in the companion TTL must appear at least once in the HTML body or KG Explorer data, or be flagged"@en ;
    schema:text """Caught 2026-07-30 on the same document: an X-post commenter ('lux') and an organization ('Oxford Semantic Technologies') were both minted as full entities in the companion TTL — with schema:name, rdfs:seeAlso/schema:url, and RDF-level links to other entities — but neither appeared ANYWHERE in the generated HTML: not in visible body prose, not in kgData for the KG Explorer. The RDF and HTML companions had silently diverged.

RULE: before delivery, enumerate every schema:Person, schema:Organization, and schema:DefinedTerm subject in the companion TTL and confirm each has at least one of: (a) a body-prose mention with a resolver link (per step6), or (b) a kgData node in the KG Explorer JS. An entity that exists only in the TTL is an orphaned entity from the reader's perspective — it was worth modeling in RDF but the HTML silently dropped it. Fix by adding a real body mention (not a token placeholder) — do not delete the RDF entity to make the counts match, since the entity is genuinely part of the source material.

BLOCKING CHECK: grep companion TTL for `a schema:Person`, `a schema:Organization`, `a schema:DefinedTerm` subject IRIs; for each, grep the HTML file (body + kgData) for the IRI or a plausible label match. Zero-hit entities must be added to the HTML before delivery."""@en .
